root_cause:
src/entities/commands.zig `createRecord()` mints a fresh random `record_id` at line ~115 on EVERY call, before it knows whether the append will be a duplicate:

    const record_uuid = blk: { var bytes: [16]u8 = undefined; fillRandom(&bytes); break :blk bytes; };
    const record_id = entities_mod.formatUuid(allocator, record_uuid) ...

`store.append()` correctly detects the idempotency-key collision and returns `AppendResult{ .is_duplicate = true, .record = <the ORIGINAL event> }`. But the duplicate branch then discards that original event entirely:

    if (append_res.is_duplicate) {
        return fetchRecordResult(allocator, conn, params.entity_type, record_id, params.tenant_id);
    }

`record_id` here is THIS call's newly-generated UUID, which was never persisted — no ENTITY_RECORD_CREATED event carries it and no entity_record_latest row has it. The lookup therefore matches zero rows and fetchRecordResult returns error.InvalidRecord.

The original record_id is available and unused: it is the `record_id` field inside `append_res.record.payload` (the first call's ENTITY_RECORD_CREATED payload, built by events_mod.buildCreatedPayload). The same defect shape exists in updateRecord/deleteRecord's duplicate branches, though there it is benign because those take record_id as an input parameter rather than minting one.
